THE BIOTECHNOLOGICAL ACCELERATION AND THE GOVERNANCE GAP: 


A THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK FOR MEASURING MISMATCHES AND DESIGNING INSTITUTIONS

Author: Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM


Abstract

This thesis develops a theoretical framework for investigating whether, when, and how governance systems fail to match the diffusion of biological engineering capabilities—and which institutional designs might reduce dangerous mismatches. It does not report empirical findings, experiments, or coded datasets. It is a work of conceptual synthesis and research design.

The thesis replaces the under-specified “governance lag” narrative with a pluralised mismatch framework distinguishing seven structural dimensions: temporal lag, scope fit, enforcement uptake, legitimacy, distributional equity, tested effectiveness, and durability. Two of these are further disaggregated in this revision. Effectiveness is split into tested effectiveness (performance under independent adversarial evaluation at a stated date) and adaptive effectiveness (patch latency and patch-deployment coverage after a disclosed evasion). Durability is scored through a de-legalisation risk composite combining legal basis, political-cycle survival, and active repeal agenda. The thesis also adds a co-production coding category to every governance event and proposes a negative-case pilot to test whether governance choices redirect or suppress capabilities rather than merely lagging them.

The framework is developed through conceptual reconstruction of three governance domains: heritable human genome editing, AI-enabled synthetic biology, and advanced therapy access. These are illustrative configurations, not tested cases. Five institutional proposals are advanced, each now accompanied by a power-and-enforcement annex specifying appointing authority, standing to challenge, sanctions for non-participation, and publication rules for dissent.

The central contribution is not a verdict on whether civilisation can govern biotechnology. It is a disciplined way of asking which governance arrangements, under which political and economic conditions, measurably make powerful biological technologies safer, fairer, and more legitimate—and a refusal to answer that question by assertion.

