Chapter 1 — Introduction: From Intuition to Falsifiable Programme

1.1 The problem beneath the problem

The decisive question is not whether humanity will acquire the capacity to edit genomes, design proteins with artificial intelligence, and engineer living systems at scale. It almost certainly will. The decisive question is whether human institutions can develop the scientific, ethical, and governance capabilities required to manage that power at a rate comparable to its development and deployment.

1.2 The analytical trap

If the hypothesis is simply “governance lags technology,” then any observed failure confirms it, and any observed success can be reinterpreted as a lag in another dimension. A hypothesis that cannot fail is not a research programme; it is a worldview.

This revision adds a second trap identified in review: treating effectiveness as a static property. A control can pass an independent test on one date and fail against an adversary who has read the test. The framework must therefore measure not only whether a control works, but how quickly it is repaired and how completely the repair is deployed.

1.3 Central research question

Under what conditions do governance systems reduce the mismatch between biological capability, safety assurance, equitable access, and public legitimacy—and which institutional mechanisms are most plausibly suited to reducing specific mismatches across contexts?

1.4 Core hypothesis and its falsification conditions

The core hypothesis is that governance failure is not a single lag but a set of specific, measurable mismatches between the diffusion of capability and the availability of effective oversight, access pathways, and accountability.

The hypothesis would be weakened if, across a systematically selected sample of governance events:

1. temporal lags were consistently zero or negative across technology tracks and jurisdictions;
2. scope-fit failures were randomly distributed rather than clustering around convergent and distributed technologies;
3. enforcement uptake were uniformly high across provider types and income levels;
4. legitimacy deficits were unrelated to the inclusiveness and framing of deliberation;
5. distributional gaps were fully explained by factors unrelated to governance design;
6. controls that exist were shown to work under independent adversarial testing at rates comparable to their formal adoption, and to be repaired and redeployed within a stated latency threshold after a disclosed evasion;
7. governance instruments were shown to survive reversal and non-implementation at rates that make durability an unnecessary dimension; and
8. governance design choices were shown to be uncorrelated with which capabilities are pursued, delayed, or abandoned — the co-production null.

These are predictions, not findings.

1.5 Guiding principle

Technological progress should be evaluated not only by the capabilities it creates, but also by the capacity of civilisation to understand, govern, and distribute those capabilities responsibly. The goal is not to slow humanity down because its power is growing. It is to help humanity become capable of handling the power it is acquiring.

