Chapter 2 — Literature Review and Theoretical Orientation

2.1 The pacing-problem literature and its limitations

The “pacing problem” was named and developed by Marchant, Allenby, and Herkert, who described a growing gap between the pace of science and technology and the lagging responsiveness of legal and ethical oversight. This literature is the necessary starting point, but it tends to treat governance as a unitary actor and technology as an exogenous trajectory.

Correction from source validation. The earlier draft attributed a pacing-problem critique to Selinger and Hartzog. Their published work concerns facial surveillance, obscurity, and consent, not the pacing problem. The critique that legal specificity, rather than technological speed, may drive the perceived lag belongs to a separate law-and-technology literature; the framework now cites that line of argument generically and does not attribute it to Selinger and Hartzog.

More recent work has begun to challenge the inevitability of lag. Kim, Kwon, Kim, and Lee trace the comparative co-evolution of synthetic-biology regulation and find differentiated pathways rather than uniform lag: national differences “stem less from institutional design choices than from the triggers that initiate regulatory change,” yielding four distinct co-evolutionary pathways and “no single optimal” pathway. The OECD’s anticipatory-governance framework similarly finds that principles are often stated in national strategies but not yet embedded in binding instruments—a gap between articulation and implementation, not necessarily a failure of foresight.

2.2 Co-production as a standing prediction, not a rival hypothesis

A rival hypothesis must be given equal standing: governance and technology are co-produced. Governance arrangements—patent regimes, funding priorities, export controls, regulatory classifications—constitute the direction and rate of technological change.

This revision strengthens the claim. The CMS Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model is not merely a payment mechanism layered onto an exogenous therapy; it defines which outcomes count, which data are collected, and which patients are tracked. The NIH’s 2025 Access Plan requirement for licensees of NIH-owned patents attempts to write equity into licensing terms before the market exists. These are not reactions to a fully formed technology. They are constitutive governance events.

The research programme must therefore code every governance event for co-productive content: did the instrument (a) merely restrict an existing capability, (b) create a new pathway or market, or (c) alter the classification of an activity in a way that changes what counts as “research” or “therapy”? If most events are (a), the lag narrative survives. If many are (b) or (c), co-production is the better description.

2.3 The seven structural dimensions

Temporal lag. The interval between a capability becoming deployable and a governance response taking effect, disaggregated by instrument type—statute, regulation, guideline, and self-governance.

Scope fit. Existing frameworks were built for pathogens, toxins, and contained laboratory risks. Gene drives, AI-enabled protein design, and autonomous biofoundries do not fit neatly into these categories. McLennan, Freemont, and Pretorius demonstrate that when synthetic biology, AI, and automation converge, regulatory friction is multiplicative rather than additive.

Enforcement and uptake. A norm can exist without being implemented. Zakaria and colleagues show that only a small fraction of more than 700 known synthesis providers worldwide maintain publicly identifiable screening mechanisms, with policy mandates and operational infrastructure absent simultaneously in many regions.

Legitimacy. A governance system can be fast and still illegitimate. The relevant indicators include who deliberated, which framings were admissible, and whether deliberation had authority to alter the outcome.

Distributional equity. A governance system can keep pace for wealthy jurisdictions while failing globally. Indicators include net price relative to income, treatment-centre density, financing coverage, and whether access conditions bind in practice.

Tested effectiveness. Whether a control works when independently tested, not merely whether it exists. Edison, Toner, and Esvelt fragmented DNA sequences and ordered them from 38 providers; 36 shipped fragments sufficient to assemble 1918 influenza, including 12 of 13 members of the International Gene Synthesis Consortium. A governance framework that counts formal adoption but not tested performance cannot distinguish assurance from appearance.

Durability. Whether an instrument survives political and legal reversal, missed deadlines, and non-implementation. The durability dimension in this revision is scored through a de-legalisation risk composite rather than a simple reversal count.

2.4 Two disaggregations demanded by the evidence

2.4.1 Effectiveness is adversarial and dynamic

The static effectiveness dimension is insufficient. The Microsoft-led Paraphrase Project found that AI-redesigned toxin variants evaded existing screening software; the team worked for ten months to develop and deploy a patch, and even after deployment approximately 3% of potentially dangerous sequences still slipped through. A snapshot pass rate cannot capture this.

Proposal adopted in this version. Replace the single effectiveness dimension with two sub-dimensions:

· Tested effectiveness: independent red-team pass rate at a stated date, with the adversarial method and the provider version disclosed.
· Adaptive effectiveness: patch latency (days between disclosed evasion and deployed update) and patch-deployment coverage (the fraction of known providers that actually installed the update).

The Paraphrase patch was voluntary and unevenly distributed; the indicator should make that unevenness visible rather than treating “a patch exists” as success.

2.4.2 Durability is de-legalisation, not just reversal

A control can exist on paper and be effectively suspended in practice. Executive Order 14292, issued on 5 May 2025, placed the 2024 Framework for Nucleic Acid Synthesis Screening on hold and directed OSTP to revise or replace it within 90 days. As of the available record, no replacement had been issued, and the policy remained paused rather than merely delayed. Separately, South Africa’s HHGE guidelines were repealed in 2025 and replaced with an indefinite placeholder, and the EU’s recent vote on new genomic techniques dismantled core safeguards on risk assessment and traceability. De-legalisation is an active strategy, not a passive risk.

Proposal adopted in this version. Add a de-legalisation risk score to the durability dimension, combining three components:

1. Legal basis: statute (lowest risk), regulation, executive guidance, voluntary multistakeholder arrangement (highest risk).
2. Political-cycle survival: whether the instrument has survived one full alternation of executive power.
3. Active repeal agenda: whether repeal or replacement is currently on a legislative or executive agenda.

The score should be published alongside the instrument count. A jurisdiction with many instruments but high de-legalisation risk is not durable; it is merely verbose.

