Chapter 4 — Heritable Human Genome Editing: Enforcement, Durability, and the Research–Reproduction Boundary

4.1 The governance landscape

Heritable human genome editing is the most mature governance domain. Baylis, Darnovsky, Hasson, and Krahn found that of 106 countries surveyed, 96 had relevant policies; 75 prohibited heritable editing (70 outright, five with exceptions), and none explicitly permitted it. Many of those prohibitions plausibly predate the 2018 He Jiankui affair. On a strict temporal-lag definition, the lag in many jurisdictions is therefore negative: governance preceded the capability’s public contestation.

This is precisely why a single lag metric is inadequate. The relevant mismatches in this domain are not primarily temporal. They are enforcement, the research–reproduction boundary, jurisdictional arbitrage, and durability.

Japan’s 2026 law, enacted on 17 July 2026, imposes penalties of up to ten years’ imprisonment or a ¥10 million fine for implanting genome-edited embryos into humans or animals, with prohibitions and penalties taking effect on 24 July 2027. This is a binding instrument, but it arrives roughly seven and a half years after November 2018—and it coexists with permissive jurisdictions elsewhere.

4.2 The research-versus-reproduction boundary

Baylis and colleagues found that 23 countries prohibit germline editing research and 11 permit it. The contested line in contemporary practice is research versus reproduction, which an “enforcement” indicator alone does not capture. A governance event may restrict reproduction while permitting research, or vice versa.

4.3 Jurisdictional arbitrage

Press reports describe ventures exploring permissive jurisdictions where embryo editing is not prohibited. A jurisdictional-arbitrage indicator would count permissive jurisdictions and named ventures reported to operate or plan work there. This is a scope-and-enforcement hybrid, not a simple lag.

4.4 Durability and de-legalisation

The ISCT, ARM, and ASGCT called for an extendable ten-year moratorium until at least 2035, but a moratorium is not a detector, a sanction, or a jurisdictional reach. The South African repeal of HHGE guidelines in 2025 illustrates the de-legalisation risk: an instrument that existed and was then withdrawn, replaced by an indefinite placeholder. The de-legalisation risk score for this domain should be high in jurisdictions where the instrument rests on guidance rather than statute, and should be tracked alongside the count of prohibition instruments.

4.5 What this configuration implies

The HHGE domain is best understood as a case in which norm articulation was rapid, binding operationalisation was uneven and delayed, and durability is untested. A composite readiness index weighted heavily on temporal lag would misdescribe this domain, because the most consequential mismatches lie in enforcement, the research–reproduction boundary, arbitrage, and de-legalisation risk.

