Chapter 5 — AI-Enabled Synthetic Biology: Scope Fit, Tested Effectiveness, and Adaptive Effectiveness

5.1 The convergence problem

The convergence of synthetic biology, AI, and automation—SynBioxAI—is the domain where the mismatch hypothesis is most plausible on its face. McLennan and colleagues surveyed sixteen nations across four regulatory domains and found that novel SynBioxAI objects fall between established categories. The governance challenge is structural: the frameworks were not designed for convergent science.

5.2 Screening asymmetry and the adoption spectrum

Zakaria and colleagues argue that narrowing global screening asymmetries requires reframing adoption as a spectrum rather than an all-or-nothing compliance threshold, and they situate screening within a layered defence framework in which multiple imperfect but complementary safeguards reduce systemic risk. This thesis adopts that reframing and credits it explicitly.

The asymmetry is severe: only a small fraction of more than 700 known providers worldwide maintain publicly identifiable screening mechanisms. This is a failure of implementation, not of norm articulation.

5.3 Tested effectiveness: the controls do not hold

Edison, Toner, and Esvelt ordered 2–8 fragments from each of 38 providers; 36 shipped fragments collectively sufficient to assemble 1918 influenza, including 12 of 13 IGSC members. The framework’s tested effectiveness indicator would record this as a near-total failure of the control as tested, regardless of the number of providers with formal screening policies.

5.4 Adaptive effectiveness: the patch is not the deployment

The Paraphrase Project provides the clearest available case for the adaptive effectiveness sub-dimension. AI-redesigned toxins evaded screening; the collaborative patch took ten months to develop and distribute, and even after deployment approximately 3% of dangerous sequences still slipped through. A governance framework that counted the patch as success without measuring its latency and coverage would have misdescribed the assurance level.

The Evo 2 case adds a further adaptive challenge: fine-tuning the open-weight model on sequences from 110 harmful human-infecting viruses partially recovers misuse-relevant predictive capabilities, meaning that data exclusion is a safeguard whose robustness under adversarial fine-tuning is an empirical question, not a settled property. The adaptive effectiveness indicator should therefore include model-safeguard robustness as a candidate measure for open-weight biological foundation models.

5.5 Durability and de-legalisation in the US screening framework

Executive Order 14292 (5 May 2025) placed the 2024 Framework for Nucleic Acid Synthesis Screening on hold and directed OSTP to revise or replace it within 90 days. No replacement had been issued as of the available record, and the policy remained paused. This is not merely “durability risk.” It is a case of executive-basis vulnerability: an instrument resting on executive action was suspended by subsequent executive action. The de-legalisation risk score should code this as high-risk on legal basis, and the framework predicts that instruments with statutory bases will survive equivalent political shocks more often.

5.6 Managed access and its limits

The NTI framework proposes tiered access to biological AI tools based on risk level, with verification of user legitimacy and equitable access as a balancing principle. NTI itself notes that risk-tier criteria still need to be developed. The framework therefore treats managed access as a promising but untested governance mechanism, and adds two requirements: that tier boundaries be tied to evaluation triggers, and that a data tier be included alongside tool-access tiers.

5.7 What this configuration implies

The SynBioxAI domain is predicted to exhibit high scope-fit friction, low tested effectiveness, and slow adaptive effectiveness, even where formal controls exist. The appropriate governance response is not blanket restriction but layered assurance with conformity assessment, recurring red-teaming, and cross-provider split-order detection.

