Chapter 6 — Advanced Therapy Access: Distribution, Durability, and Implementation Instability

6.1 The equity gap as a governance failure

Gene and cell therapies illustrate a form of governance failure that is invisible to a safety-only lens. The same product carries different national prices: US list prices for sickle-cell gene therapies are approximately US$2.2 million (Casgevy) and US$3.1 million (Lyfgenia) per patient, while national pricing and reimbursement decisions in other jurisdictions produce different effective prices. This dispersion is evidence about national price governance, not a single global price.

6.2 India’s NexCAR19: a governance success with a delivery gap

NexCAR19 was launched in India in April 2024 at an initial price of ₹42 lakh per dose, which subsequently fell to approximately ₹30 lakh, and it has been administered to over 350 patients across 70 hospitals. The earlier draft’s ₹42 lakh figure is therefore retained but correctly sequenced: it was the initial price, not the current price. The cost reduction is real, but it is a market and manufacturing achievement as much as a governance achievement. Regulatory approval and tax exemption contributed, but delivery capacity and financing remain binding constraints.

Source grade: D (press and company reporting; no peer-reviewed costing study identified).

6.3 Outcomes-based payment as a live test — and an instability signal

The CMS Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model is not an untested proposal. It is a live test of outcomes-based agreements for Medicaid sickle-cell therapies. The state count is internally inconsistent across CMS’s own primary sources: the July 2025 announcement and subsequent coverage report 33 states, the District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico, representing approximately 84% of Medicaid beneficiaries with sickle-cell disease, while a 2026 CMS FAQ page states 32 states plus DC and Puerto Rico.

This inconsistency is not a footnote to be resolved away. It is evidence for the durability and implementation dimension: a flagship access model cannot produce a stable participant count across its own official communications. The framework should record the discrepancy as an implementation-instability indicator, not choose one number and discard the other.

Source grade: B (government primary sources, internally conflicting).

6.4 What this configuration implies

The advanced-therapy domain is predicted to exhibit high distributional mismatch, moderate durability risk, and implementation instability, because access conditions are often added after market authorisation rather than built into funding. The relevant governance question is not whether a therapy is approved, but whether the conditions attached to public support bind in practice—and whether the programme’s own reporting can be relied upon.

