Chapter 7 — Cross-Domain Propositions and Predictions

These propositions are predictions, not findings.

Proposition 1 (dimensional heterogeneity). Across domains, the dominant mismatch will differ: temporal lag will be least explanatory in HHGE; scope fit, tested effectiveness, and adaptive effectiveness will dominate in SynBioxAI; and distribution plus implementation instability will dominate in advanced therapies.

Proposition 2 (lead-and-lag within the same instrument). Korea’s Synthetic Biology Promotion Act, enacted in April 2025 and effective in April 2026, is a promotion-led statute. Korean-language government and news sources confirm that Article 25 concerns synthetic-biology R&D guidelines and Article 26 concerns the safety-management system, with detail deferred to subordinate legislation. The Act therefore leads on legal foundation and lags on operational specificity. A composite index would miss this pattern.

Proposition 3 (assurance illusion). The fraction of providers with formal screening will exceed the fraction whose screening withstands independent adversarial testing. Edison et al.’s 36-of-38 result is consistent with this prediction but does not establish its generality.

Proposition 4 (adaptive latency). Patch latency after a disclosed evasion will be measured in months, not days, and patch-deployment coverage will be incomplete even among providers that formally subscribe to the screening norm. The Paraphrase ten-month patch and ~3% residual evasion rate are consistent with this prediction.

Proposition 5 (de-legalisation clusters by legal basis). Instruments resting on executive action or voluntary arrangements will be suspended or repealed more often than statutory instruments. EO 14292’s suspension of the 2024 Framework is a case consistent with this prediction, but one case does not establish it.

Proposition 6 (co-production is visible in negative cases). At least some capabilities will be shown to have been delayed, redirected, or abandoned because of governance design choices, not merely because of technological difficulty. The negative-case pilot in §3.6 is the test.

