Chapter 9 — Discussion: Co-Production, Power, and the Limits of a Theoretical Framework

9.1 Co-production as a standing prediction

The framework must not naturalise the gap by treating technology as the independent variable. This revision therefore adds a co-production null to the falsification conditions (§1.4, condition 8) and a coding category to every governance event (§2.2, §3.4). If co-production is the better description, the negative-case pilot (§3.6) will show it.

9.2 The politics of indicators

Indicators are not neutral. The CMS 32/33 discrepancy is a live example: the same programme produces two participant counts in its own primary sources. A dashboard that resolves the discrepancy by choosing one number would obscure an implementation-instability signal. The framework treats indicator instability as data, not noise.

9.3 The risk of governance fetishism

The framework must avoid the opposite error of assuming that more governance is always better. The criterion is fit-for-purpose governance, not maximal governance. Each proposal is stated with its failure condition and its power annex, not only its rationale.

9.4 Evidence grades and the limits of the present synthesis

Most of the evidence assembled here is recent: perspectives, policy papers, preprints, and press. The thesis grades its sources accordingly. It does not claim that the propositions in Chapter 7 are established. It claims that they are worth testing, and it specifies how.

