Chapter 10 — Conclusion: A Programme, Not a Verdict

The central contribution of this thesis is not a verdict on whether humanity can govern biotechnology. It is a research architecture for asking which governance arrangements, under which political and economic conditions, measurably make powerful biological technologies safer, fairer, and more legitimate without unnecessarily preventing their benefits.

The framework replaces a single, unfalsifiable “lag” narrative with seven dimensions, two of which are disaggregated into tested and adaptive effectiveness, and durability is scored through a de-legalisation risk composite. It treats co-production as a standing prediction rather than a caveat. It states predictions and specifies what evidence would count against them. It designs pilots, including a negative-case pilot, without pretending to have run them. It proposes institutions with failure conditions and power annexes attached.

The goal is not to slow humanity down because its power is growing. It is to help humanity become capable of handling the power it is acquiring. That is a civilisational task, and it deserves a research programme as disciplined as the technologies it seeks to govern.

