Appendix A — Governance Event Coding Manual (v2.0)

This manual defines the unit of analysis and the coding decisions that must be made before any empirical execution. Every governance event in the sampling frame is coded on the dimensions below. Codes are assigned at the event level, not at the level of the technology or the institution as a whole.

A.1 Unit definition

A governance event is a specific instrument, decision, or non-decision addressed to a specific capability in a specific jurisdiction at a specific date. Examples: a statute receiving royal assent; a regulatory guidance document issued; a voluntary moratorium announced by a professional society; a funding condition imposed by a public agency; a decision not to classify an activity as regulated. Events are dated by the date on which the instrument takes effect, not the date of announcement, unless the instrument itself is the announcement (e.g., a voluntary moratorium).

A.2 Core coding fields

Field Code options Notes
Event ID Jurisdiction-capability-year-number Unique identifier
Jurisdiction ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 plus “INT” for international bodies 
Capability HHGE-research; HHGE-reproduction; SynBioxAI-synthesis; SynBioxAI-AI-design; AT-access Capability, not technology sector
Instrument type Statute; regulation; guideline; voluntary self-governance; funding condition; classification decision Disaggregation required by §2.3
Temporal lag Months between capability deployability and event effect date Negative values permitted (governance led)
Scope fit 1–5 ordinal; 5 = capability clearly within existing category Coding rule in A.4
Enforcement uptake 0–100% of known providers/actors subject to implementation Source must state denominator
Legitimacy 1–5 ordinal; 5 = inclusive deliberation with authority to alter outcome Coding rule in A.5
Distributional equity 0–3 ordinal; 3 = access conditions bind in practice Coding rule in A.6
Tested effectiveness 0–100% pass rate under independent adversarial evaluation Date and evaluator must be recorded
Adaptive effectiveness Patch latency in days; deployment coverage % Two sub-fields; see A.7
Durability: legal basis Statute=1; regulation=2; guidance=3; voluntary=4 Lower score = more durable
Durability: political-cycle survival Yes/No/Not yet tested 
Durability: active repeal agenda Yes/No/Unknown 
Co-production code R = restriction; C = market/pathway creation; X = classification change See §2.2
Source grade A/B/C/D See A.8

A.3 Temporal lag coding rules

The capability deployability date must be justified by a source of Grade A or B. For HHGE, the reference date is November 2018 (the He Jiankui announcement); for SynBioxAI screening, the reference date is the first commercial availability of benchtop synthesisers or AI protein-design tools, whichever is earlier and documented; for advanced therapies, the reference date is the first regulatory approval in any jurisdiction. A lag of zero or negative is coded as “governance led” and must be flagged in the dataset. The hypothesis is weakened if the median lag is zero or negative in at least half of sampled jurisdictions for a track.

A.4 Scope-fit coding rule

Scope fit is coded 1 when the capability is not addressed by any existing regulatory category and no classification decision has been made; 2 when the capability falls between two categories and actors report uncertainty about which applies; 3 when a classification decision exists but is contested; 4 when a classification decision exists and is accepted by regulated actors; 5 when the capability is clearly and uncontroversially within an existing category. The coding must cite the specific regulatory text or authoritative interpretation. McLennan and colleagues’ finding that SynBioxAI objects “fall between established regulatory categories” is the anchor for the low end of this scale .

A.5 Legitimacy coding rule

Legitimacy is not the same as public approval. It is coded on four sub-criteria: (i) whether deliberation occurred before classification; (ii) whether affected parties were represented; (iii) whether the admissible framings included ethical and distributional questions, not only risk-benefit; and (iv) whether the deliberative body had authority to alter the classification. Each sub-criterion is coded yes/no; the ordinal score is the count of yes answers (0–4). A body that deliberates but cannot change the outcome receives a maximum of 2.

A.6 Distributional equity coding rule

Equity is coded 0 when no access conditions are attached to public support; 1 when access plans are required but not monitored; 2 when access plans are monitored but sanctions are discretionary; 3 when access conditions bind through enforceable sanctions or payment linkage. The CMS Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model is the live test case: it codes as 2 or 3 depending on whether outcomes-based rebates are actually triggered and whether state participation is stable .

A.7 Adaptive effectiveness coding rule

Two fields must be recorded separately. Patch latency is the number of days between the first public disclosure of a screening evasion and the deployment of a patch by at least one major provider. Deployment coverage is the fraction of known providers that have installed the patch, with the denominator stated. The Microsoft Paraphrase case illustrates why both fields are necessary: the patch took approximately 10 months to develop and distribute, and even after deployment a residual fraction of dangerous sequences remained undetected . A single “patch exists” code would have obscured both the latency and the residual gap.

A.8 Source grading rules

Grade Definition Examples in this thesis
A Peer-reviewed empirical study with transparent methods Edison et al. 2026; Haider et al. 2020; Baylis et al. 2020
B Official government or intergovernmental primary document; may contain internal inconsistency EO 14292; CMS FAQ; Japan law text
C Peer-reviewed commentary, perspective, or secondary summary of primary text Zakaria et al. 2026; McLennan et al. 2026; Korean-language news summaries of Articles 25–26
D Press, preprint, company blog, or advocacy document NexCAR19 pricing reports; Microsoft Signal blog

A Grade D source may be used for domain detail but may not be the sole basis for a falsification claim. When a Grade B source is internally inconsistent, the inconsistency is recorded as data (see Appendix D.2).

