B.2 Source-grade field requirement

The source grade must be entered for every field that supports a numeric claim. If a numeric claim rests on a Grade D source, the field must be flagged GRADE_D_CONFIRM_REQUIRED and must not be used in a hypothesis test until confirmed by a Grade A or B source. This rule applies to the NexCAR19 ₹42 lakh initial price (Grade D; see Appendix D.3) and to the Korean Articles 25–26 safety-content claim (Grade C; see Appendix D.4).

B.3 Inter-coder agreement protocol

At least 20% of events must be double-coded. Agreement is reported as Krippendorff’s alpha for ordinal fields and as percentage agreement for categorical fields. A field with alpha below 0.67 is treated as under-specified and returned to the coding manual for revision. This threshold is pre-registered; it cannot be adjusted after coding begins.

B.4 Confounder fields

For every event, the coder records whether the following confounders are present: (i) the jurisdiction is a high-income country; (ii) the capability is primarily commercial rather than research-oriented; (iii) the event occurred after a focusing tragedy or scandal; (iv) the event was initiated by a regional or international body rather than domestically. These fields are not used to adjust the main scores; they are used in interaction testing, as required by §3.4.

